WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.303
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 05 of the

2
00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:07.125
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Devices and Endpoints.

3
00:00:07.125 --> 00:00:10.901
It's for the architects and
engineers who move a domain-joined

4
00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:13.862
fleet onto Intune, and its
servers onto Azure Arc.

5
00:00:13.862 --> 00:00:16.936
It comes in two parts of about
twenty-five minutes each,

6
00:00:16.936 --> 00:00:19.326
and four modules you can watch separately.

7
00:00:19.326 --> 00:00:21.037
Part 1 is the endpoints.

8
00:00:21.037 --> 00:00:24.869
First, how a new device is governed
from the moment it powers on,

9
00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:29.075
and the three paths an existing estate
can take to the same end state.

10
00:00:29.075 --> 00:00:34.330
Then the Group Policy sunset: how
every GPO is inventoried, classified,

11
00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:38.950
replaced and retired, and the
policy library that replaces it.

12
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.102
Part 2 is servers and the run state:

13
00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:44.715
bringing servers under Azure
Arc with a day-one baseline,

14
00:00:44.715 --> 00:00:49.788
and then operating and evidencing the
whole fleet once Intune is the authority.

15
00:00:49.788 --> 00:00:53.538
The slides show tables,
flows and decision matrices.

16
00:00:53.538 --> 00:00:57.390
The scripts and policy payloads live
in the chapters and in Appendix A;

17
00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:01.774
they're marked there as illustrative and
untested against a production tenant,

18
00:01:01.774 --> 00:01:05.120
and I'll point you to them
rather than put them on screen.

19
00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.058
Why this matters.

20
00:01:07.058 --> 00:01:09.589
For twenty years Group
Policy did a great deal

21
00:01:09.589 --> 00:01:12.120
of governance without
anyone writing it down.

22
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:16.785
A computer sat in an organizational
unit (OU), GPOs linked to that OU

23
00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:21.795
and its parents applied in a fixed
order, and the closest container won.

24
00:01:21.795 --> 00:01:25.738
That one structure was the targeting
model, the precedence model,

25
00:01:25.738 --> 00:01:29.607
the delegation model, a timing
guarantee and an evidence source.

26
00:01:29.607 --> 00:01:34.652
Microsoft's planning guidance says it
plainly: Group Policy enforcement isn't

27
00:01:34.652 --> 00:01:39.696
possible on cloud-native endpoints, which
are joined to Entra ID, not to a domain.

28
00:01:39.696 --> 00:01:43.870
Intune can deliver most of the
settings, but each mechanism changes.

29
00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.783
Assignment to groups is flat.

30
00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:48.919
Assignment filters evaluate
device properties at

31
00:01:48.919 --> 00:01:52.057
enrollment and check-in, up
to two hundred per tenant.

32
00:01:52.057 --> 00:01:56.076
A platform script runs once and
times out after thirty minutes;

33
00:01:56.076 --> 00:01:59.632
Remediations rerun every
twenty-four hours by default.

34
00:01:59.632 --> 00:02:02.270
There's no built-in
answer for mapped drives.

35
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:06.432
Devices check in about every eight
hours instead of every ninety minutes.

36
00:02:06.432 --> 00:02:10.370
And per-setting status is reported
after check-in, so it can lag.

37
00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.207
The conflict rules change too.

38
00:02:12.207 --> 00:02:16.310
When two Intune configuration
policies set one setting differently,

39
00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:19.846
the conflict is reported and the
setting may not apply at all.

40
00:02:19.846 --> 00:02:22.932
Compliance policies take
the most restrictive value.

41
00:02:22.932 --> 00:02:27.540
And on a hybrid device that gets
both a GPO and an MDM policy,

42
00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.844
Group Policy wins by default.

43
00:02:29.844 --> 00:02:35.079
MDM wins over GP reverses that only
for the Policy CSP; for other CSPs,

44
00:02:35.079 --> 00:02:40.407
such as Defender, Microsoft warns of
a race with no guaranteed winner.

45
00:02:40.407 --> 00:02:43.483
So the program's rule is
regenerate, not copy.

46
00:02:43.483 --> 00:02:49.195
Microsoft's own guidance is not to lift
and shift: identify the mandatory settings,

47
00:02:49.195 --> 00:02:53.160
compare them with MDM, and
treat the gaps as blockers.

48
00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:58.260
Two more ideas from the first part of the
book frame everything in this session.

49
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.398
First, the hybrid-join trap.

50
00:03:00.398 --> 00:03:05.247
Converting a domain-joined fleet to hybrid
join is close to a tenant-wide switch.

51
00:03:05.247 --> 00:03:08.081
Extend the sync scope to the computer OUs,

52
00:03:08.081 --> 00:03:11.582
publish a service connection
point in each forest,

53
00:03:11.582 --> 00:03:16.417
or push the same two values by GPO,
and every Windows device's Automatic

54
00:03:16.417 --> 00:03:21.085
Device Join task reads it and
registers itself, retrying every hour.

55
00:03:21.085 --> 00:03:25.673
Nothing in that path checks ownership,
organizational position or approval.

56
00:03:25.673 --> 00:03:30.138
Microsoft's guidance has two halves:
Entra join is the default for new,

57
00:03:30.138 --> 00:03:35.466
refurbished or reset devices; hybrid join
gives existing domain-joined devices a

58
00:03:35.466 --> 00:03:40.559
cloud identity with minimal impact, but it
shouldn't be long term or the end goal.

59
00:03:40.559 --> 00:03:44.408
Converting a hybrid device to
Entra join later needs a reset,

60
00:03:44.408 --> 00:03:48.872
and hybrid devices need periodic line
of sight to a domain controller.

61
00:03:48.872 --> 00:03:52.485
Done ungoverned, the waypoint
becomes the destination.

62
00:03:52.485 --> 00:03:54.623
Second, where the truth lives.

63
00:03:54.623 --> 00:04:00.231
A modern laptop is described by a procurement
record, an Autopilot registration,

64
00:04:00.231 --> 00:04:04.751
an Entra ID device object, an Intune
record and a Defender machine;

65
00:04:04.751 --> 00:04:06.760
a server adds an Arc resource.

66
00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:11.148
The program decides, fact by
fact, which one is authoritative.

67
00:04:11.148 --> 00:04:13.522
Procurement owns the business facts.

68
00:04:13.522 --> 00:04:16.597
The Entra object owns
identity and join type.

69
00:04:16.597 --> 00:04:20.071
Intune owns management
and evaluates compliance;

70
00:04:20.071 --> 00:04:22.884
Entra's is-compliant flag only reports it.

71
00:04:22.884 --> 00:04:25.273
Defender owns threat and exposure.

72
00:04:25.273 --> 00:04:27.260
Arc owns management for servers.

73
00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:31.975
Correlate on the right key: the Entra
device ID across Microsoft records,

74
00:04:31.975 --> 00:04:36.297
the serial number to procurement,
and the Azure resource ID for Arc.

75
00:04:36.297 --> 00:04:38.346
And delete the Entra object last,

76
00:04:38.346 --> 00:04:42.350
because deleting it deletes
the BitLocker recovery keys.

77
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.912
Module 1: Intune-first
onboarding and the paths.

78
00:04:46.912 --> 00:04:51.412
Migration takes a device that already
exists and brings it under governance.

79
00:04:51.412 --> 00:04:56.363
Intune-first refuses to let an ungoverned
device exist in the first place.

80
00:04:56.363 --> 00:05:01.543
Chapter 04 covers devices bought after
the modernization decision, net-new only;

81
00:05:01.543 --> 00:05:05.641
the program keeps the phrase
Intune-first for that doctrine and

82
00:05:05.641 --> 00:05:09.662
doesn't use it for replacing Group
Policy on the existing fleet.

83
00:05:09.662 --> 00:05:14.148
Chapter 05 then covers how existing
workstations and servers reach

84
00:05:14.148 --> 00:05:18.850
the same end state, by one of three
paths that share a single runbook.

85
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.562
The doctrine is simple to state.

86
00:05:21.562 --> 00:05:25.582
A net-new asset is governed from
the moment it first powers on:

87
00:05:25.582 --> 00:05:29.038
by Intune for endpoints, and
by Azure Arc for servers.

88
00:05:29.038 --> 00:05:33.298
There is no state where it joins
on-premises Active Directory to wait for a

89
00:05:33.298 --> 00:05:37.697
migration, gets provisioned by
Configuration Manager and co-managed later,

90
00:05:37.697 --> 00:05:40.281
runs without an organizational assignment,

91
00:05:40.281 --> 00:05:43.563
or reaches production without
a compliance evaluation.

92
00:05:43.563 --> 00:05:47.103
Five pillars carry it, and
they're the program's doctrine,

93
00:05:47.103 --> 00:05:48.950
not Microsoft requirements.

94
00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:53.650
No ungoverned device, ever;
quarantine is the safety valve.

95
00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.854
Procurement is the first governance
step: the assignment is set when

96
00:05:57.854 --> 00:06:01.763
the purchase order is issued, the
way HR feeds user provisioning.

97
00:06:01.763 --> 00:06:03.099
Zero-touch only.

98
00:06:03.099 --> 00:06:05.210
One management plane per asset,

99
00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:09.837
so no greenfield Configuration
Manager, Group Policy or hybrid join.

100
00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:13.546
And quarantine on failure: an
onboarding failure produces a

101
00:06:13.546 --> 00:06:17.625
quarantine entry and a ticket,
never a degraded production device.

102
00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:20.737
Every asset follows the same five phases.

103
00:06:20.737 --> 00:06:25.251
Procure, with the assignment validated
against the organizational registry.

104
00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:28.087
Pre-stage, where the
vendor record carries it.

105
00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.464
Position, where the governance pipeline

106
00:06:30.464 --> 00:06:33.050
commits a mapping keyed
on the serial number.

107
00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:36.249
Note that phase 3 doesn't
pre-stage an Entra object;

108
00:06:36.249 --> 00:06:40.462
classic Autopilot creates that
automatically at registration,

109
00:06:40.462 --> 00:06:44.675
so phase 3 confirms the registration
carries the right group tag.

110
00:06:44.675 --> 00:06:46.512
Provision, zero-touch.

111
00:06:46.512 --> 00:06:47.600
And validate.

112
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:52.116
The assignment comes from the first
source that can supply it: procurement,

113
00:06:52.116 --> 00:06:55.394
then the vendor carrier, then
derivation from the user,

114
00:06:55.394 --> 00:06:58.162
and finally an explicit
unpositioned marker.

115
00:06:58.162 --> 00:07:01.524
A known nowhere is still
a value a rule can match.

116
00:07:01.524 --> 00:07:05.650
The latency target is fourteen
calendar days from purchase order,

117
00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.660
five for expedited orders.

118
00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:12.060
Microsoft now ships two
Windows provisioning solutions.

119
00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:17.403
Windows Autopilot device preparation is a
re-architecture with simpler configuration

120
00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:22.385
and near real-time reporting; classic
Windows Autopilot remains more feature-rich.

121
00:07:22.385 --> 00:07:25.135
The rows on the left are
the differences that matter.

122
00:07:25.135 --> 00:07:29.697
Device preparation needs no
registration, so it has no group tag.

123
00:07:29.697 --> 00:07:33.885
It can't do hybrid join,
pre-provisioning or self-deploying mode.

124
00:07:33.885 --> 00:07:38.692
It applies up to ten apps and ten scripts
during setup, can't hold the desktop

125
00:07:38.692 --> 00:07:43.498
until user configuration lands, and
doesn't use the Enrollment Status Page.

126
00:07:43.498 --> 00:07:48.985
But it's available in GCC High and
DoD, where classic Autopilot isn't.

127
00:07:48.985 --> 00:07:53.960
The workbook assumes a
Microsoft 365 GCC tenant.

128
00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.693
Intune has no separate GCC instance:

129
00:07:56.693 --> 00:08:02.160
GCC uses the commercial Intune service,
so both solutions are available.

130
00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.835
Only GCC High and DoD use the
separate government instance.

131
00:08:06.835 --> 00:08:12.468
The program's default for standard hardware
in GCC is classic Windows Autopilot,

132
00:08:12.468 --> 00:08:18.185
user-driven, with Entra join, and OEM or
reseller registration carrying a group tag.

133
00:08:18.185 --> 00:08:19.760
The reason is pillar 2.

134
00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.074
Classic Autopilot's registration
carries the procurement attribute,

135
00:08:24.074 --> 00:08:26.413
the group tag and the purchase order ID,

136
00:08:26.413 --> 00:08:30.873
from the vendor to the Entra device
object before the device is powered on.

137
00:08:30.873 --> 00:08:34.203
Device preparation adds devices
to a group at enrollment,

138
00:08:34.203 --> 00:08:38.573
so the assignment has to come from the
procurement mapping table afterward.

139
00:08:38.573 --> 00:08:41.989
Use device preparation
where its advantages matter,

140
00:08:41.989 --> 00:08:46.648
such as faster provisioning or a tenant
that might later move to GCC High.

141
00:08:46.648 --> 00:08:57.235
It needs Windows 11 24H2, or 23H2 or 22H2
with KB5035942, and automatic enrollment.

142
00:08:57.235 --> 00:09:00.547
And the first user must be
allowed to join devices:

143
00:09:00.547 --> 00:09:04.748
on that path, the user's own join
permission brings the device in.

144
00:09:04.748 --> 00:09:08.280
Never use either with hybrid
join for new devices.

145
00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.476
Here's how the procurement
decision reaches a

146
00:09:11.476 --> 00:09:14.243
device's configuration
without anyone touching it.

147
00:09:14.243 --> 00:09:18.793
The purchase order carries an organizational
node code and the serial number.

148
00:09:18.793 --> 00:09:23.450
The OEM, reseller or distributor
registers the hardware hash at purchase,

149
00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:25.817
which is also what makes it zero-touch.

150
00:09:25.817 --> 00:09:31.118
A supplier can register devices only after
the organization's Global Administrator

151
00:09:31.118 --> 00:09:35.598
authorizes it; Microsoft calls out
that this one feature needs that role,

152
00:09:35.598 --> 00:09:39.555
so plan the consent as a one-time,
recorded approval per supplier.

153
00:09:39.555 --> 00:09:44.469
The group tag Intune stores on the
registration appears on the Entra device

154
00:09:44.469 --> 00:09:48.993
as an Order ID physical ID, and the
purchase order as a Purchase Order ID.

155
00:09:48.993 --> 00:09:52.809
A dynamic group rule matches on
them, and the Autopilot profile,

156
00:09:52.809 --> 00:09:56.331
compliance and configuration
are assigned to that group.

157
00:09:56.331 --> 00:10:00.473
The program's convention is that
the tag holds the node identifier,

158
00:10:00.473 --> 00:10:02.583
never a free-text department name,

159
00:10:02.583 --> 00:10:07.555
so a reorganization changes the mapping
table, not thousands of registrations.

160
00:10:07.555 --> 00:10:11.180
Enrollment restrictions give the
doctrine its enforcement point.

161
00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:15.940
Block personally owned Windows enrollment,
and Intune authorizes corporate

162
00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:19.967
enrollment only through approved
paths, of which Autopilot is one.

163
00:10:19.967 --> 00:10:24.094
Device preparation devices are
blocked unless they're pre-identified

164
00:10:24.094 --> 00:10:27.592
with corporate identifiers or
bound by device association.

165
00:10:27.592 --> 00:10:32.051
An unregistered device then fails
with an enrollment restriction error,

166
00:10:32.051 --> 00:10:33.805
which is pillar 5 in practice.

167
00:10:33.805 --> 00:10:40.080
Naming: fifteen characters or fewer, letters,
numbers and hyphens, not all numbers.

168
00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:44.311
The program uses a short asset-class
prefix plus the serial macro,

169
00:10:44.311 --> 00:10:47.367
because the serial is
the procurement join key.

170
00:10:47.367 --> 00:10:49.031
Position goes in the tag.

171
00:10:49.031 --> 00:10:53.411
And the Enrollment Status Page: use
a custom one, block on a Selected

172
00:10:53.411 --> 00:10:58.380
list of security-critical items, and
don't let users bypass it for those.

173
00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.729
A net-new device should arrive at a known

174
00:11:01.729 --> 00:11:04.643
baseline before anyone
uses it, in two halves.

175
00:11:04.643 --> 00:11:06.592
Compliance decides access.

176
00:11:06.592 --> 00:11:08.571
First set the tenant-wide setting,

177
00:11:08.571 --> 00:11:12.455
mark devices with no compliance
policy assigned as Not compliant,

178
00:11:12.455 --> 00:11:16.269
so an unassigned device can't pass
Conditional Access by default;

179
00:11:16.269 --> 00:11:20.718
Microsoft recommends this whenever
compliance feeds Conditional Access.

180
00:11:20.718 --> 00:11:26.174
The Windows policy typically requires
BitLocker, Secure Boot and code integrity;

181
00:11:26.174 --> 00:11:28.903
firewall, TPM, antivirus and antispyware;

182
00:11:28.903 --> 00:11:33.879
current Defender intelligence with
real-time protection; and a minimum OS.

183
00:11:33.879 --> 00:11:38.030
With Defender for Endpoint connected,
add a machine risk score ceiling.

184
00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:40.305
Configuration sets the device up.

185
00:11:40.305 --> 00:11:43.123
Start from the Windows security baseline,

186
00:11:43.123 --> 00:11:47.792
version 25H2 being the latest, and
the Defender for Endpoint baseline.

187
00:11:47.792 --> 00:11:52.009
Existing baseline profiles don't
move to a new version automatically,

188
00:11:52.009 --> 00:11:56.954
and different baselines can set the same
setting differently, so review overlaps.

189
00:11:56.954 --> 00:11:59.708
Then BitLocker with escrow to Entra ID,

190
00:11:59.708 --> 00:12:03.580
Windows Hello for Business,
firewall and Windows LAPS.

191
00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:08.043
Because these devices never had Group
Policy, there's nothing to conflict with.

192
00:12:08.043 --> 00:12:09.193
That's the payoff.

193
00:12:09.193 --> 00:12:12.792
Four exception paths cover what
can't follow the main path,

194
00:12:12.792 --> 00:12:17.517
each with written justification, a
compensating control and an audit entry.

195
00:12:17.517 --> 00:12:21.130
A: personal devices,
governed at lower assurance.

196
00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:24.630
B: Linux that Intune
can't manage, under Arc.

197
00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:29.160
C: Server 2019 and 2022, under
Arc without Entra sign-in,

198
00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:32.455
which is Microsoft's support boundary.

199
00:12:32.455 --> 00:12:38.168
D: Windows that can't enroll, such as
instrument PCs or disconnected enclaves.

200
00:12:38.168 --> 00:12:41.143
Path D can't pass a
compliant-device policy.

201
00:12:41.143 --> 00:12:42.654
So isolate it first.

202
00:12:42.654 --> 00:12:46.315
Manage its Defender settings through
security settings management,

203
00:12:46.315 --> 00:12:47.955
which doesn't make it compliant.

204
00:12:47.955 --> 00:12:52.853
And if its users must reach a protected
resource, record a named exclusion, paired

205
00:12:52.853 --> 00:12:57.960
with a policy that blocks that group from
every device not on the approved list.

206
00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:02.460
Chapter 05 is about how the
existing estate gets there.

207
00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:07.953
The workbook's sources described three
separate kits that repeated most of the same

208
00:13:07.953 --> 00:13:13.210
steps, because the destination is the same:
servers in Arc, workstations in Intune,

209
00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:17.604
NTLM restricted after it's measured,
clean service principal names,

210
00:13:17.604 --> 00:13:21.135
hardened SQL Server, and
Conditional Access enforced.

211
00:13:21.135 --> 00:13:23.635
What differs is who holds
the decision record.

212
00:13:23.635 --> 00:13:29.235
The rule all three obey: the control
plane governs, the data plane executes.

213
00:13:29.235 --> 00:13:35.060
The program holds desired state,
observes, classifies drift and reconciles.

214
00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:40.660
Entra ID, Intune, Azure Policy and
Active Directory do the runtime work.

215
00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:45.535
An adapter may call a management API;
it never sits inline in a sign-in.

216
00:13:45.535 --> 00:13:47.790
Every path runs four phases in order,

217
00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:51.097
and nothing is restricted
until it has been measured.

218
00:13:51.097 --> 00:13:52.786
Observe is read-only.

219
00:13:52.786 --> 00:13:55.460
Onboard attaches the management planes.

220
00:13:55.460 --> 00:14:00.048
Harden closes the identity gaps,
only after Phase 1 is signed off.

221
00:14:00.048 --> 00:14:02.523
Watch keeps the estate from drifting back.

222
00:14:02.523 --> 00:14:07.299
Budget at least two weeks of assessment,
treat every first run as a dry run,

223
00:14:07.299 --> 00:14:10.325
deploy Conditional Access
report-only first,

224
00:14:10.325 --> 00:14:14.385
and exclude the emergency access
accounts before enforcing.

225
00:14:14.385 --> 00:14:16.947
Domain unjoin is a separate decision.

226
00:14:16.947 --> 00:14:22.222
The manual path suits small estates and
pilots; the operator owns the change record.

227
00:14:22.222 --> 00:14:27.309
The governed path is for larger estates, or
where an authorizing official must sign:

228
00:14:27.309 --> 00:14:30.847
every state-changing step
carries an approval reference.

229
00:14:30.847 --> 00:14:35.048
The retrofit adopts a hand-modernized
estate without re-enrollment.

230
00:14:35.048 --> 00:14:37.804
Its risk is that registering
the current state

231
00:14:37.804 --> 00:14:40.348
as desired blesses
whatever drift is there.

232
00:14:40.348 --> 00:14:42.810
Every governed operation declares a rung.

233
00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:48.660
The federal ceiling is L3, human
approval, and autonomous L4 is refused.

234
00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:54.280
Where Intune Multi Admin Approval covers
a workload, it is the L3 approval.

235
00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:58.842
Three places in the shared
runbook cause most of the outages.

236
00:14:58.842 --> 00:15:00.117
Precedence first.

237
00:15:00.117 --> 00:15:05.703
Moving the existing fleet into Intune
usually goes through Group Policy triggered

238
00:15:05.703 --> 00:15:10.968
automatic enrollment of hybrid joined
devices: no reimage, no user interaction.

239
00:15:10.968 --> 00:15:16.293
Confirm with dsregcmd that the device shows
both Azure AD joined and domain joined.

240
00:15:16.293 --> 00:15:20.192
But while the GPOs are still
linked, overlaps are dangerous.

241
00:15:20.192 --> 00:15:24.617
When the same policy is set in
both, Group Policy takes precedence.

242
00:15:24.617 --> 00:15:29.229
MDM wins over GP reverses
that only for the Policy CSP.

243
00:15:29.229 --> 00:15:32.705
For the Defender CSP
and others, it's a race.

244
00:15:32.705 --> 00:15:36.259
And Windows Hello for Business,
in the PassportForWork CSP,

245
00:15:36.259 --> 00:15:40.755
won't take the Intune settings until
the Group Policy settings are cleared.

246
00:15:40.755 --> 00:15:44.955
So document every overlap before
Intune enforces anything.

247
00:15:44.955 --> 00:15:46.917
NTLM second.

248
00:15:46.917 --> 00:15:49.888
It's deprecated in all versions,

249
00:15:49.888 --> 00:15:55.143
and NTLMv1 is removed in
Windows 11 24H2 and Server 2025.

250
00:15:55.143 --> 00:16:00.405
A common mistake is treating LAN
Manager level 5 as block all NTLM.

251
00:16:00.405 --> 00:16:07.129
Level 5 makes domain controllers refuse LM
and NTLMv1, but they still accept NTLMv2.

252
00:16:07.129 --> 00:16:10.930
Blocking is done by the
Restrict NTLM policies.

253
00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:14.956
So stage it: audit, refuse the
legacy variants, then deny all,

254
00:16:14.956 --> 00:16:18.395
removing exceptions as
each dependency retires,

255
00:16:18.395 --> 00:16:23.680
and only after a fourteen to thirty day
audit and application owner sign-off.

256
00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:26.080
Conditional Access can't help here:

257
00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:30.880
a Kerberos or NTLM exchange with a
domain controller never reaches it.

258
00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:32.130
SPNs third.

259
00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:36.898
A missing SPN is the silent
case: Kerberos isn't attempted,

260
00:16:36.898 --> 00:16:41.767
and NTLM usually succeeds, until
stage 3 makes it an outage.

261
00:16:41.767 --> 00:16:43.817
A duplicate is the loud case.

262
00:16:43.817 --> 00:16:46.848
Validate each repair by
purging the ticket cache

263
00:16:46.848 --> 00:16:50.100
and checking that SQL
Server reports Kerberos.

264
00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:54.938
Module 2: the GPO sunset
and policy migration.

265
00:16:54.938 --> 00:16:57.038
This is the existing-fleet program.

266
00:16:57.038 --> 00:17:01.604
It replaces Group Policy Objects on
endpoints that are already deployed,

267
00:17:01.604 --> 00:17:04.599
and it ends when those
GPOs are formally retired.

268
00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:09.712
The program measures it by GPOs retired
with evidence, not by settings copied.

269
00:17:09.712 --> 00:17:11.774
Chapter 07 runs it as a program,

270
00:17:11.774 --> 00:17:17.462
Chapter 08 is what an endpoint engineer
works from inside those rules, and Appendix

271
00:17:17.462 --> 00:17:23.090
A is the crosswalk: where a control lands
in Intune, not what its value should be.

272
00:17:23.840 --> 00:17:27.659
Three efforts touch Intune and
the program keeps them apart:

273
00:17:27.659 --> 00:17:32.470
Intune-first onboarding for new devices,
this sunset for the existing fleet,

274
00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:37.053
and regenerate-not-copy, the philosophy
for authoring the replacements.

275
00:17:37.053 --> 00:17:42.563
Microsoft's own guidance is that assessing
every GPO and every setting is likely

276
00:17:42.563 --> 00:17:47.827
impractical; identify the mandatory
settings and treat the gaps as blockers.

277
00:17:47.827 --> 00:17:52.266
The program turns that into seven
steps, each with an exit condition.

278
00:17:52.266 --> 00:17:56.740
Step 1: nothing is translated or
retired until it's inventoried.

279
00:17:56.740 --> 00:18:02.565
Get-GPO-Report produces each GPO's
links, security filtering, WMI filtering

280
00:18:02.565 --> 00:18:08.015
and settings as XML, which is the
shape Group Policy analytics imports.

281
00:18:08.015 --> 00:18:12.577
Record the targeting too, because it's
the part that doesn't survive the move.

282
00:18:12.577 --> 00:18:17.218
The program's triage script sorts
each live GPO onto a target plane,

283
00:18:17.218 --> 00:18:22.787
Intune for workstations and Arc for servers,
using two classifiers side by side,

284
00:18:22.787 --> 00:18:27.090
so disagreements surface instead
of hiding behind one guess.

285
00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:31.940
Group Policy analytics shows, per
setting, the CSP and OMA-URI behind it,

286
00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:37.040
and sorts settings into ready for
migration, not supported or deprecated.

287
00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:42.112
Plan around its limits: each export
under four megabytes with proper Unicode,

288
00:18:42.112 --> 00:18:47.105
the five CSPs plus preferences it
parses, non-ADMX settings read accurately

289
00:18:47.105 --> 00:18:51.940
only in English, and scope tags that
hide imports from admins without them.

290
00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:56.028
The Migrate step is, in
Microsoft's words, best effort.

291
00:18:56.028 --> 00:18:59.077
Firewall rules and AppLocker
can't move that way.

292
00:18:59.077 --> 00:19:04.038
And when several GPOs set one setting
differently, migration stops and asks

293
00:19:04.038 --> 00:19:09.077
you to pick, which usefully surfaces the
conflicts OU precedence used to hide.

294
00:19:09.077 --> 00:19:12.532
The program treats the readiness
report as evidence that

295
00:19:12.532 --> 00:19:15.627
an equivalent exists, never
that a setting is needed.

296
00:19:15.627 --> 00:19:19.530
And it quotes no percentage:
Microsoft publishes none.

297
00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:23.855
Step 2 gives every setting
in the inventory one tag.

298
00:19:23.855 --> 00:19:28.230
Required: still needed, so it's
translated to a modern control.

299
00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:31.876
Obsolete: an old Windows
version, a retired product,

300
00:19:31.876 --> 00:19:35.955
a network assumption that no
longer holds, so it's retired.

301
00:19:35.955 --> 00:19:41.280
High-risk: it weakens security, so it's
retired or replaced with something stronger.

302
00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:44.066
Redundant: already enforced by another

303
00:19:44.066 --> 00:19:47.105
control or a Windows
default, so it's retired.

304
00:19:47.105 --> 00:19:51.178
A required setting then gets a
disposition from four questions,

305
00:19:51.178 --> 00:19:53.868
asked in order, where
the first yes decides.

306
00:19:53.868 --> 00:19:56.680
Is it still required in
the target operating model?

307
00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:01.180
Group Policy accumulated settings that
existed only because it could set them.

308
00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:03.742
Does a native Intune equivalent exist?

309
00:20:03.742 --> 00:20:04.655
Most do.

310
00:20:04.655 --> 00:20:07.005
Does a cloud-native
pattern remove the need?

311
00:20:07.005 --> 00:20:10.908
Folder redirection becomes
OneDrive Known Folder Move,

312
00:20:10.908 --> 00:20:16.305
mapped printers become Universal Print,
WSUS targeting becomes update rings.

313
00:20:16.305 --> 00:20:18.343
And can a script enforce and prove it?

314
00:20:18.343 --> 00:20:22.230
Then it's a Remediation, reviewed
later for a native replacement.

315
00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:25.480
A setting that fails all
four is a dated exception,

316
00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:29.205
and its GPO stays linked for
that population and no other.

317
00:20:29.205 --> 00:20:32.646
For each one, also record
whether it's configuration,

318
00:20:32.646 --> 00:20:35.767
compliance, or both, and
what evidence proves it.

319
00:20:35.767 --> 00:20:38.829
The questions send settings
to four landing zones.

320
00:20:38.829 --> 00:20:43.064
The settings catalog holds most
ADMX and security settings;

321
00:20:43.064 --> 00:20:48.992
the old Administrative Templates profile
has been read-only since the 2412 release.

322
00:20:48.992 --> 00:20:53.576
Imported ADMX is for third-party
templates: public preview,

323
00:20:53.576 --> 00:20:56.943
twenty files of a megabyte
or less, en-us only.

324
00:20:56.943 --> 00:21:02.717
Endpoint security owns antivirus, encryption,
firewall, attack surface reduction,

325
00:21:02.717 --> 00:21:08.156
account protection and EDR, and Microsoft's
practice is one method per setting.

326
00:21:08.156 --> 00:21:12.397
Remediations need Enterprise,
Education or VDA licensing,

327
00:21:12.397 --> 00:21:15.440
up to two hundred packages per tenant.

328
00:21:16.190 --> 00:21:19.605
While a population has both
GPOs and Intune policies,

329
00:21:19.605 --> 00:21:24.340
three conflict systems are in play,
and the program plans against each one.

330
00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:28.983
Inside Intune: two compliance policies
take the most restrictive value;

331
00:21:28.983 --> 00:21:33.548
a compliance setting beats the same
setting in a configuration policy;

332
00:21:33.548 --> 00:21:37.804
and between configuration policies
of any type, settings catalog,

333
00:21:37.804 --> 00:21:41.828
endpoint security, security
baselines, there is no precedence.

334
00:21:41.828 --> 00:21:45.990
The conflict is reported and the setting
might not apply until it's fixed.

335
00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:49.924
The chapter states two corrections
to earlier program material:

336
00:21:49.924 --> 00:21:52.401
profiles don't resolve by last write wins,

337
00:21:52.401 --> 00:21:56.990
and endpoint security doesn't
automatically beat configuration profiles.

338
00:21:56.990 --> 00:22:00.190
So each setting has
exactly one owning policy.

339
00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:03.782
Between Group Policy and
MDM: in the Policy CSP,

340
00:22:03.782 --> 00:22:09.738
Group Policy keeps winning unless MDM wins
over GP is set to one; set it where a

341
00:22:09.738 --> 00:22:15.977
population is in transition and remove
the GPO copy as Intune takes the setting.

342
00:22:15.977 --> 00:22:18.119
Outside the Policy CSP it's a race,

343
00:22:18.119 --> 00:22:23.039
and Windows Hello for Business shouldn't
be configured through both at all.

344
00:22:23.039 --> 00:22:28.888
For Windows Update, Microsoft
recommends Ignore Windows Update

345
00:22:28.888 --> 00:22:34.952
Group Policies instead, on Windows
11 24H2 with KB5101684 and later.

346
00:22:34.952 --> 00:22:37.604
Between Configuration Manager and Intune,

347
00:22:37.604 --> 00:22:41.140
the co-management slider sets
one authority per workload.

348
00:22:41.140 --> 00:22:46.231
Note two traps: switching device
configuration also moves resource access and

349
00:22:46.231 --> 00:22:49.625
Endpoint Protection; and
with Pilot Intune selected,

350
00:22:49.625 --> 00:22:53.790
Intune deploys policies but doesn't
remove them on unassignment.

351
00:22:53.790 --> 00:22:58.178
Disabling a link is the moment production
behavior changes, so it's gated.

352
00:22:58.178 --> 00:23:03.012
Freeze the GPO, back it up, prove
the replacement reports success,

353
00:23:03.012 --> 00:23:07.665
then disable the link, not the
GPO, with one reversible command.

354
00:23:07.665 --> 00:23:10.275
And remember that preference
items stay on the

355
00:23:10.275 --> 00:23:13.320
device unless configured
to remove themselves.

356
00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:18.445
Chapter 08 breaks the seven
phases into working tasks.

357
00:23:18.445 --> 00:23:23.043
The durations come from the program's
reference plan for a large fleet with an

358
00:23:23.043 --> 00:23:27.857
existing Configuration Manager estate; a
fleet without one skips most of Phase 2.

359
00:23:27.857 --> 00:23:32.015
Phase 1, three to five weeks:
export and import every GPO,

360
00:23:32.015 --> 00:23:37.834
find the GPO-on-GPO conflicts that OU
precedence hid, classify every setting,

361
00:23:37.834 --> 00:23:44.208
and capture an effective-policy baseline
per device tier with gpresult on a sample.

362
00:23:44.208 --> 00:23:49.456
Phase 2, two to three weeks: enable
co-management for a pilot collection,

363
00:23:49.456 --> 00:23:55.608
set the first workloads to Pilot Intune,
and decide MDM wins over GP per population.

364
00:23:55.608 --> 00:23:58.865
The matching Intune policy
must already exist,

365
00:23:58.865 --> 00:24:03.795
because a co-managed device syncs MDM
policy as soon as a workload moves.

366
00:24:03.795 --> 00:24:08.700
Phase 3, three to four weeks, can overlap
it: build every policy unassigned,

367
00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:14.342
with attack surface reduction and controlled
folder access in audit mode, and peer

368
00:24:14.342 --> 00:24:19.983
review that every setting traces to a
decision record and no setting appears twice.

369
00:24:19.983 --> 00:24:25.292
Phase 4 is the pilot, about five percent
of the fleet, representative across tiers,

370
00:24:25.292 --> 00:24:29.808
regions and departments, with a
seven-day grace period instead of one.

371
00:24:29.808 --> 00:24:33.135
GPOs stay linked: the
pilot measures conflicts by

372
00:24:33.135 --> 00:24:36.707
comparing gpresult with
Intune's per-setting status.

373
00:24:36.707 --> 00:24:41.671
Phase 5 is four waves of three weeks
each, watched for seventy-two hours.

374
00:24:41.671 --> 00:24:45.906
Endpoint Protection goes before
device configuration, because moving

375
00:24:45.906 --> 00:24:50.495
device configuration drags Endpoint
Protection and resource access with it.

376
00:24:50.495 --> 00:24:56.061
Phases 6 and 7 disable the remaining
links, clear tattooed preferences,

377
00:24:56.061 --> 00:25:01.532
then disable each GPO, keep it ninety
days, back it up, archive and delete.

378
00:25:01.532 --> 00:25:06.137
On these estimates the program reaches
Phase 7 in roughly thirty-four weeks,

379
00:25:06.137 --> 00:25:09.133
with the last deletions
about three months later.

380
00:25:09.133 --> 00:25:12.473
Remember that rolling back
a pilot means reassigning a

381
00:25:12.473 --> 00:25:16.380
policy with the old value, not
just removing the assignment.

382
00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:24.185
The policy library has five types, each
with an ID prefix: compliance policies, CP;

383
00:25:24.185 --> 00:25:28.568
settings catalog profiles,
SC; endpoint security,

384
00:25:28.568 --> 00:25:32.630
ES; app protection, AP;
and app configuration, AC.

385
00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:37.191
Every one is assigned to dynamic
device groups built from attributes,

386
00:25:37.191 --> 00:25:39.393
which replaces OU-linked scoping.

387
00:25:39.393 --> 00:25:44.434
Entra dynamic rules for devices can use
extension attributes one through fifteen,

388
00:25:44.434 --> 00:25:48.818
but the values have to be written to
the device object through Microsoft

389
00:25:48.818 --> 00:25:52.617
Graph first; they're the device's
own set, not the user attributes.

390
00:25:52.617 --> 00:25:56.959
The program uses one numbering: one
for region, three for department,

391
00:25:56.959 --> 00:26:00.292
eleven for security tier,
twelve for device role and

392
00:26:00.292 --> 00:26:04.168
thirteen for environment, with
one fixed spelling per value.

393
00:26:04.168 --> 00:26:08.135
Assignments then add exclude groups
for break-glass and staging,

394
00:26:08.135 --> 00:26:09.605
and assignment filters.

395
00:26:09.605 --> 00:26:13.967
The correction that matters: filters
can't read extension attributes.

396
00:26:13.967 --> 00:26:17.068
A filter on attribute
eleven doesn't evaluate.

397
00:26:17.068 --> 00:26:21.189
Attribute targeting belongs in
the group rule; filters work on

398
00:26:21.189 --> 00:26:25.629
Intune properties such as join type,
ownership, model and OS edition.

399
00:26:25.629 --> 00:26:28.992
Three Microsoft facts shape
every compliance template.

400
00:26:28.992 --> 00:26:32.063
Noncompliance actions
are scheduled in days,

401
00:26:32.063 --> 00:26:34.892
zero to three hundred
sixty-five, not hours.

402
00:26:34.892 --> 00:26:40.020
Windows supports only three actions:
mark noncompliant, email the user,

403
00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:44.192
and add to the retire list,
which retires nothing by itself.

404
00:26:44.192 --> 00:26:45.660
There is no block action;

405
00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:49.917
blocking is what Conditional Access
does with a noncompliant device.

406
00:26:49.917 --> 00:26:54.300
And BitLocker, Secure Boot and code
integrity are measured at boot,

407
00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:57.168
so a newly encrypted
device needs a restart.

408
00:26:57.168 --> 00:27:01.234
The set: a workstation
baseline with a 24H2 floor;

409
00:27:01.234 --> 00:27:04.486
a strict Tier 0 policy with a 25H2 floor,

410
00:27:04.486 --> 00:27:10.381
a Clear risk score and no grace period;
a Tier 2 policy; and a kiosk policy.

411
00:27:10.381 --> 00:27:18.100
The server baseline, CP-004, can't be
built in Intune, so it moves to Chapter 09.

412
00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:22.366
Endpoint security policies
aren't a higher-priority source;

413
00:27:22.366 --> 00:27:25.600
they have the same standing
as configuration profiles.

414
00:27:25.600 --> 00:27:31.300
In the program's library they own
every Defender, BitLocker, firewall,

415
00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:37.600
account protection and LAPS setting,
and the four settings catalog IDs that

416
00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:43.100
duplicated them, SC-002, 006, 007 and
008, are retired and never reused.

417
00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:49.044
ES-001, antivirus: cloud block level
High, a fifty-second extended timeout,

418
00:27:49.044 --> 00:27:53.800
which is the maximum, and safe
samples sent automatically.

419
00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:57.950
Sending all samples can upload
documents with organizational data,

420
00:27:57.950 --> 00:27:59.988
so that's the data owner's decision.

421
00:27:59.988 --> 00:28:03.903
Tamper protection needs Defender
onboarding and isn't supported

422
00:28:03.903 --> 00:28:07.676
this way on co-managed devices
until Endpoint Protection moves.

423
00:28:07.676 --> 00:28:14.117
ES-002, BitLocker: silent encryption and
a TPM PIN can't apply to the same device,

424
00:28:14.117 --> 00:28:19.250
so standard and kiosk devices
encrypt silently with TPM only,

425
00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:23.175
and Tier 0 uses TPM plus
PIN in a separate policy.

426
00:28:23.175 --> 00:28:28.572
ES-003, firewall: on an Entra-joined
device the domain profile doesn't apply,

427
00:28:28.572 --> 00:28:32.642
so management rules need a
private-profile equivalent,

428
00:28:32.642 --> 00:28:36.800
and Group Policy analytics
can't migrate firewall rules.

429
00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:40.015
ES-004, EDR: automated investigation,

430
00:28:40.015 --> 00:28:47.087
live response and web content filtering
are set in the Defender portal, not here.

431
00:28:47.087 --> 00:28:52.990
ES-005: attack surface reduction rules
in audit for thirty days, then block;

432
00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:58.215
the PSExec and WMI rule stays off
Configuration Manager devices,

433
00:28:58.215 --> 00:29:00.925
because its client depends on WMI.

434
00:29:00.925 --> 00:29:05.559
ES-006: Credential Guard, which
needs Enterprise or Education,

435
00:29:05.559 --> 00:29:11.737
Hello for Business, and Windows LAPS with
an eight-hour post-authentication reset.

436
00:29:11.737 --> 00:29:14.038
Appendix A flags the traps.

437
00:29:14.038 --> 00:29:17.700
Account lockout maps to the
DeviceLock lockout policy.

438
00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:20.875
Audit Logon sits under
the logon-logoff path.

439
00:29:20.875 --> 00:29:24.075
A logon message breaks
Autopilot pre-provisioning.

440
00:29:24.075 --> 00:29:28.120
And Known Folder Move won't carry
folders redirected to a share:

441
00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:31.050
copy them first with Migration Manager.

442
00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:33.969
Module 3: servers under Azure Arc.

443
00:29:33.969 --> 00:29:37.423
Group Policy reached servers
through OU placement,

444
00:29:37.423 --> 00:29:39.913
the same way it reached workstations.

445
00:29:39.913 --> 00:29:41.933
Intune doesn't take over that job,

446
00:29:41.933 --> 00:29:46.125
because Intune's supported platforms
don't include Windows Server.

447
00:29:46.125 --> 00:29:48.513
The cloud equivalent is Azure Arc.

448
00:29:48.513 --> 00:29:52.613
A server outside Azure runs the
Connected Machine agent and appears

449
00:29:52.613 --> 00:29:56.499
as an Azure resource, managed the
way native virtual machines are.

450
00:29:56.499 --> 00:29:59.441
Arc gives the server an
address in the control plane;

451
00:29:59.441 --> 00:30:03.970
everything that makes it governed is
what you then attach to that address.

452
00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:06.383
Arc is the management plane.

453
00:30:06.383 --> 00:30:09.976
It isn't the identity plane, and
onboarding doesn't change how

454
00:30:09.976 --> 00:30:13.857
the server authenticates users or
whether it's joined to a domain.

455
00:30:13.857 --> 00:30:16.195
Keep the two separate in every plan.

456
00:30:16.195 --> 00:30:21.366
What changes: the server gets an Azure
resource ID in a resource group alongside

457
00:30:21.366 --> 00:30:25.753
other Azure resources; Azure RBAC,
tags and Azure Policy apply to it;

458
00:30:25.753 --> 00:30:30.298
it gets a system-assigned managed
identity in Entra ID; and extensions

459
00:30:30.298 --> 00:30:34.920
can be installed from Azure, running
with full privileges on the server.

460
00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:40.120
What doesn't: its domain membership; how
users and services authenticate to it,

461
00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:43.288
unless you enable Entra
ID sign-in; the workload,

462
00:30:43.288 --> 00:30:47.432
since an Arc outage impairs
management only; and where it runs.

463
00:30:47.432 --> 00:30:49.408
Arc isn't a migration to Azure.

464
00:30:49.408 --> 00:30:52.045
Two properties shape the operating model.

465
00:30:52.045 --> 00:30:54.640
Azure is the source of
truth for the agent:

466
00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:58.837
the only way to tell it to do something
is to act on the server's Azure

467
00:30:58.837 --> 00:31:01.355
representation, so RBAC and policy are

468
00:31:01.355 --> 00:31:04.408
evaluated before any
change reaches the server.

469
00:31:04.408 --> 00:31:06.657
And connection state is evidence.

470
00:31:06.657 --> 00:31:09.545
The agent sends a heartbeat
every five minutes.

471
00:31:09.545 --> 00:31:13.764
A server that stops shows Disconnected
within fifteen to thirty minutes,

472
00:31:13.764 --> 00:31:17.222
and one disconnected for forty-five
days can become Expired,

473
00:31:17.222 --> 00:31:21.233
unmanageable until an administrator
disconnects and reconnects it.

474
00:31:21.233 --> 00:31:25.758
If a server disconnects, its guest
assignments are kept for fourteen days

475
00:31:25.758 --> 00:31:30.432
and then deleted, so a long outage
silently removes its baseline evaluation.

476
00:31:30.432 --> 00:31:33.196
Plan cost by service, not by connection.

477
00:31:33.196 --> 00:31:36.804
If the Windows Server licenses
carry Software Assurance,

478
00:31:36.804 --> 00:31:38.985
or are subscription or pay-as-you-go,

479
00:31:38.985 --> 00:31:43.195
Windows Server Management enabled
by Arc provides Update Manager,

480
00:31:43.195 --> 00:31:47.857
Change Tracking and Inventory and
machine configuration at no extra cost.

481
00:31:47.857 --> 00:31:51.506
But it isn't automatic: you
attest each server's licensing,

482
00:31:51.506 --> 00:31:54.890
and that becomes a recorded
step in the runbook.

483
00:31:55.640 --> 00:32:00.752
Before the first server connects,
settle roles, network, tags and images.

484
00:32:00.752 --> 00:32:02.989
Use the narrowest built-in roles.

485
00:32:02.989 --> 00:32:08.280
Azure Connected Machine Onboarding goes to
the onboarding service principal only: it

486
00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:13.277
can read and create Arc server resources,
but not delete them or manage extensions.

487
00:32:13.277 --> 00:32:17.765
Azure Connected Machine Resource Administrator
is for the server operations team.

488
00:32:17.765 --> 00:32:22.538
And Virtual Machine Administrator
Login or User Login are for people

489
00:32:22.538 --> 00:32:27.065
who sign in with Entra ID; Owner and
Contributor don't confer sign-in.

490
00:32:27.065 --> 00:32:29.127
Then remember what extensions can do.

491
00:32:29.127 --> 00:32:31.990
They run arbitrary scripts
with full privileges,

492
00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:36.457
so Microsoft says to treat any Contributor
on the Arc resource as an indirect

493
00:32:36.457 --> 00:32:40.714
administrator of the server, and the
Cloud Adoption Framework warns that

494
00:32:40.714 --> 00:32:43.715
Contributor, Owner and
the resource administrator

495
00:32:43.715 --> 00:32:46.228
role can effectively delegate root access.

496
00:32:46.228 --> 00:32:49.139
Scope those roles to the
resource groups a team owns,

497
00:32:49.139 --> 00:32:52.253
and hold them through
Privileged Identity Management.

498
00:32:52.253 --> 00:32:58.641
Network: the agent communicates outbound
over TCP 443, with no inbound rule.

499
00:32:58.641 --> 00:33:03.738
Allow the Azure Active Directory,
Traffic Manager, Resource Manager, Arc

500
00:33:03.738 --> 00:33:06.966
Infrastructure and
Storage service tags and,

501
00:33:06.966 --> 00:33:09.940
as of April 2026, Azure
Front Door Frontend.

502
00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:14.642
A proxy doesn't add security, because
the traffic is already encrypted;

503
00:33:14.642 --> 00:33:17.965
a private link scope keeps
it off public networks.

504
00:33:17.965 --> 00:33:21.250
Run azcmagent check from a
representative server first,

505
00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:24.115
adding the cloud parameter
for Azure Government.

506
00:33:24.115 --> 00:33:28.568
Tags: one taxonomy at connection
time, which drives policy scope,

507
00:33:28.568 --> 00:33:32.265
Update Manager's dynamic
scoping and cost reporting.

508
00:33:32.265 --> 00:33:34.703
They're plain text, so nothing sensitive.

509
00:33:34.703 --> 00:33:38.338
A server with SQL Server
installed connects its instances

510
00:33:38.338 --> 00:33:41.752
to Arc automatically; opt out
with a tag when you connect.

511
00:33:41.752 --> 00:33:45.077
And images: install the
agent in a golden image,

512
00:33:45.077 --> 00:33:50.150
but never connect the image, or
every clone shares one resource ID.

513
00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:52.688
Onboarding runs in waves.

514
00:33:52.688 --> 00:33:57.675
Each wave creates its credential, connects
a defined set of servers, attaches the

515
00:33:57.675 --> 00:33:59.983
baseline on the same change, verifies,

516
00:33:59.983 --> 00:34:02.887
and closes by removing
what onboarding needed.

517
00:34:02.887 --> 00:34:07.716
Microsoft supports many methods: a
portal-generated script, PowerShell,

518
00:34:07.716 --> 00:34:11.659
a service principal with the
installation script, Ansible,

519
00:34:11.659 --> 00:34:15.764
Arc-enabled VMware vSphere, the
multicloud connector for AWS,

520
00:34:15.764 --> 00:34:19.949
Windows Admin Center, Configuration
Manager, and Group Policy.

521
00:34:19.949 --> 00:34:24.621
The program's recommendation is a dedicated
service principal that authenticates

522
00:34:24.621 --> 00:34:26.889
with a certificate, deployed through the

523
00:34:26.889 --> 00:34:29.425
automation tool the
server team already runs.

524
00:34:29.425 --> 00:34:33.387
The interactive script is for a
handful of servers or a pilot.

525
00:34:33.387 --> 00:34:36.809
The service principal exists
only to connect machines,

526
00:34:36.809 --> 00:34:40.752
and the credential is needed only
while azcmagent connect runs;

527
00:34:40.752 --> 00:34:43.950
if it expires afterward,
the server stays connected.

528
00:34:43.950 --> 00:34:46.788
So keep its lifetime
short and its scope small:

529
00:34:46.788 --> 00:34:49.850
the onboarding role, on
the target resource group.

530
00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:52.676
Microsoft recommends a
certificate over a secret,

531
00:34:52.676 --> 00:34:57.050
because a certificate credential can
be controlled with Conditional Access.

532
00:34:57.050 --> 00:35:01.723
If you must use a secret, Microsoft's
examples create one valid for a year;

533
00:35:01.723 --> 00:35:04.575
set it shorter and rotate
it after each wave.

534
00:35:04.575 --> 00:35:08.979
A stolen onboarding credential lets an
attacker connect servers outside your

535
00:35:08.979 --> 00:35:13.250
organization into your subscription,
and private endpoints close that path.

536
00:35:13.250 --> 00:35:15.312
Protect the credential in deployment.

537
00:35:15.312 --> 00:35:17.910
A secret in a hand-built startup script is

538
00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:20.813
readable by every domain
user through SYSVOL.

539
00:35:20.813 --> 00:35:26.486
Microsoft's Group Policy method encrypts
its configuration file so only computer

540
00:35:26.486 --> 00:35:30.844
accounts can decrypt it, which
still means any computer account,

541
00:35:30.844 --> 00:35:36.188
so scope the GPO, rotate after the wave,
and disable the GPO once servers connect.

542
00:35:36.188 --> 00:35:40.495
For sensitive servers, set local
controls that Azure can't override:

543
00:35:40.495 --> 00:35:44.620
an extension allowlist, and
disabling remote connections.

544
00:35:45.370 --> 00:35:48.633
A connected server is
addressable, not yet governed.

545
00:35:48.633 --> 00:35:51.107
Attach three things on the same change.

546
00:35:51.107 --> 00:35:56.394
Govern with Azure machine configuration,
formerly Azure Policy Guest Configuration.

547
00:35:56.394 --> 00:36:01.055
It extends Azure Policy inside the
operating system, using Desired State

548
00:36:01.055 --> 00:36:06.020
Configuration to audit or set OS settings,
and reports compliance per machine.

549
00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:10.045
Unlike Group Policy it's scoped
by Azure resource scope,

550
00:36:10.045 --> 00:36:13.632
subscription, resource
group or machine, not by OU.

551
00:36:13.632 --> 00:36:18.207
The agent includes the service natively,
so there's no extension to deploy.

552
00:36:18.207 --> 00:36:23.196
The Windows and Linux audit baselines are
built-in definitions against the Azure

553
00:36:23.196 --> 00:36:26.057
compute security baseline,
both customizable,

554
00:36:26.057 --> 00:36:29.358
and a CIS benchmark definition
for Linux is in preview.

555
00:36:29.358 --> 00:36:32.064
Deploy the prerequisite initiative first,

556
00:36:32.064 --> 00:36:36.875
keep the customized baseline in the
governance repository as policy-as-code,

557
00:36:36.875 --> 00:36:40.708
and treat noncompliance as a
finding, not a failed deployment.

558
00:36:40.708 --> 00:36:42.833
Patch with Azure Update Manager.

559
00:36:42.833 --> 00:36:46.843
It assesses every twenty-four hours
when periodic assessment is on,

560
00:36:46.843 --> 00:36:51.856
patches in maintenance windows, and groups
machines by tag through dynamic scoping,

561
00:36:51.856 --> 00:36:54.720
which is where the patch
group tag earns its keep.

562
00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:56.972
It's charged per Arc server per month,

563
00:36:56.972 --> 00:37:00.350
except when the server is
enabled for ESUs through Arc,

564
00:37:00.350 --> 00:37:03.954
when Defender for Servers Plan
2 covers the subscription,

565
00:37:03.954 --> 00:37:08.083
or with Software Assurance, a
subscription or pay-as-you-go license.

566
00:37:08.083 --> 00:37:10.145
Protect with Defender for Servers.

567
00:37:10.145 --> 00:37:15.326
Plan 1 centers on endpoint detection and
response; Plan 2 adds agentless scanning,

568
00:37:15.326 --> 00:37:19.136
regulatory compliance, premium
vulnerability management,

569
00:37:19.136 --> 00:37:22.870
configuration assessment and
file integrity monitoring.

570
00:37:22.870 --> 00:37:26.270
The program's FedRAMP
Moderate scope uses Plan 2,

571
00:37:26.270 --> 00:37:29.670
plus Defender for SQL on
Arc-connected SQL hosts.

572
00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:33.355
Onboard through Arc rather than
installing Defender directly,

573
00:37:33.355 --> 00:37:35.232
which gives only Plan 1 function.

574
00:37:35.232 --> 00:37:39.322
And if your only Defender entitlement
comes through Defender for Servers,

575
00:37:39.322 --> 00:37:42.260
security settings
management isn't available.

576
00:37:43.010 --> 00:37:45.320
Arc is also how many organizations keep

577
00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:48.260
end-of-support servers
patched while they migrate.

578
00:37:48.260 --> 00:37:54.479
As of October twentieth, 2026: the
Windows Server 2012 and 2012 R2 ESU period

579
00:37:54.479 --> 00:38:00.698
ended on October thirteenth, and Arc
ESU licenses deactivated the next day.

580
00:38:00.698 --> 00:38:03.018
Any such host still running receives no

581
00:38:03.018 --> 00:38:05.760
further security updates
through any channel.

582
00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:08.783
Treat it as a red finding:
migrate, upgrade,

583
00:38:08.783 --> 00:38:12.072
or isolate it and record
the risk acceptance.

584
00:38:12.072 --> 00:38:17.125
Windows Server 2016 reaches end of
support on January twelfth, 2027,

585
00:38:17.125 --> 00:38:20.823
with ESUs for up to three
years; connect and enroll

586
00:38:20.823 --> 00:38:24.973
those hosts before then if they
won't be upgraded in time.

587
00:38:24.973 --> 00:38:30.919
SQL Server 2014 is in its third
ESU year, until July 2027,

588
00:38:30.919 --> 00:38:36.073
and SQL Server 2016 in its
first, until July 2029.

589
00:38:36.073 --> 00:38:38.454
ESUs through Arc are billed monthly,

590
00:38:38.454 --> 00:38:41.710
need no product keys, and
show coverage per server.

591
00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:43.472
Entra ID sign-in.

592
00:38:43.472 --> 00:38:47.994
Arc-enabled Windows servers can
use Entra ID for Remote Desktop,

593
00:38:47.994 --> 00:38:50.723
with Azure RBAC deciding who signs in.

594
00:38:50.723 --> 00:38:57.835
It needs Windows Server 2025 or later with
Desktop Experience, or Windows 11 24H2;

595
00:38:57.835 --> 00:39:03.461
2019 and 2022 aren't supported,
which is exception C from Module 1.

596
00:39:03.461 --> 00:39:06.735
It's available in Azure
Global and Azure Government.

597
00:39:06.735 --> 00:39:10.197
Installing the sign-in extension
Entra-joins the server,

598
00:39:10.197 --> 00:39:12.923
so it's for servers that
won't be domain-joined.

599
00:39:12.923 --> 00:39:16.111
Only member users of the
same tenant can sign in.

600
00:39:16.111 --> 00:39:20.847
And Conditional Access isn't supported
with the join extension on Arc servers.

601
00:39:20.847 --> 00:39:25.806
That corrects a common assumption: the
control you get is RBAC on who may sign in,

602
00:39:25.806 --> 00:39:29.186
held through PIM, not
workstation Conditional Access.

603
00:39:29.186 --> 00:39:33.860
Linux uses SSH through Arc with its
own extension and the same roles.

604
00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:37.014
Rollback order matters:
remove extensions first,

605
00:39:37.014 --> 00:39:41.399
because uninstalling the agent
leaves them behind; then disconnect,

606
00:39:41.399 --> 00:39:45.680
which needs the resource
administrator role; then uninstall.

607
00:39:46.430 --> 00:39:49.968
Module 4: operating and
evidencing the fleet.

608
00:39:49.968 --> 00:39:55.430
Once the GPOs are gone, a device's state
is no longer explained by where it sits.

609
00:39:55.430 --> 00:40:00.580
It has to be proved, continuously, from
records the services already keep.

610
00:40:00.580 --> 00:40:04.165
The run state replaces the OU
answer with three records:

611
00:40:04.165 --> 00:40:08.842
the policy library from Chapter 08,
which says what each device should be;

612
00:40:08.842 --> 00:40:12.038
the status Intune and
Defender report per device,

613
00:40:12.038 --> 00:40:15.467
which says what it is; and
the audit log of every change.

614
00:40:15.467 --> 00:40:19.130
Operating the fleet means
keeping those three in agreement.

615
00:40:19.130 --> 00:40:22.515
Evidencing it means handing
an assessor the same records,

616
00:40:22.515 --> 00:40:24.980
not a document written for the audit.

617
00:40:25.730 --> 00:40:29.363
The compliance policies decide
whether a device is compliant;

618
00:40:29.363 --> 00:40:33.205
Conditional Access decides what
a noncompliant device can reach.

619
00:40:33.205 --> 00:40:35.805
Book 04 owns that second decision.

620
00:40:35.805 --> 00:40:40.309
This book's side of the hand-off is making
sure the compliance signal is accurate,

621
00:40:40.309 --> 00:40:43.517
and remember that the
compliant-device grant works only

622
00:40:43.517 --> 00:40:46.793
for devices registered in Entra
ID and enrolled in Intune.

623
00:40:46.793 --> 00:40:49.868
Two tenant settings
sit above every policy.

624
00:40:49.868 --> 00:40:53.993
Devices with no compliance policy
assigned default to Compliant;

625
00:40:53.993 --> 00:40:58.568
the program sets Not compliant, so a
device that falls outside every group,

626
00:40:58.568 --> 00:41:03.443
a missing tier attribute for example,
can't pass Conditional Access by default.

627
00:41:03.443 --> 00:41:07.771
And the compliance status validity
period defaults to thirty days,

628
00:41:07.771 --> 00:41:11.868
with a range of one to a hundred
twenty; the program uses fourteen.

629
00:41:11.868 --> 00:41:16.649
That's a program choice: short enough that
a device left in a drawer loses access

630
00:41:16.649 --> 00:41:19.664
before its posture is stale
by a whole patch cycle,

631
00:41:19.664 --> 00:41:22.018
long enough not to catch staff on leave.

632
00:41:22.018 --> 00:41:23.880
Read the dashboard carefully.

633
00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:26.203
In grace period means noncompliant,

634
00:41:26.203 --> 00:41:29.843
but inside the schedule on
the mark noncompliant action.

635
00:41:29.843 --> 00:41:34.917
Not evaluated is a new device, or one that
hasn't checked in since a policy changed.

636
00:41:34.917 --> 00:41:38.541
A compliance setting that
returns Error keeps the existing

637
00:41:38.541 --> 00:41:42.454
status for up to seven days, then
the device becomes noncompliant.

638
00:41:42.454 --> 00:41:45.571
The program reviews Not
evaluated and Error weekly,

639
00:41:45.571 --> 00:41:48.905
because both hide devices
from the noncompliant count.

640
00:41:48.905 --> 00:41:53.288
Configuration status is per device
and per setting: Succeeded, Error,

641
00:41:53.288 --> 00:41:58.055
or Conflict, which means another policy
sets the same setting differently.

642
00:41:58.055 --> 00:42:00.593
A conflict is a defect in the library.

643
00:42:00.593 --> 00:42:03.667
Move the setting into the
one policy that owns it.

644
00:42:03.667 --> 00:42:08.614
And on Tier 0, the risk score requirement
means a Defender detection on a privileged

645
00:42:08.614 --> 00:42:11.624
access workstation becomes
a compliance failure,

646
00:42:11.624 --> 00:42:14.840
and through Conditional
Access, a loss of access.

647
00:42:15.590 --> 00:42:20.990
Intune manages Windows updates through four
policy types built on the Windows Update

648
00:42:20.990 --> 00:42:25.265
client policies: update rings for
deferrals, deadlines and restarts;

649
00:42:25.265 --> 00:42:30.440
feature update policies that pin a Windows
version; quality update policies, which

650
00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:35.540
can expedite a critical update or deliver
hotpatches; and driver update policies.

651
00:42:35.540 --> 00:42:38.127
Rings stage each month's quality update.

652
00:42:38.127 --> 00:42:41.710
The names follow Windows
Autopatch, Test, First,

653
00:42:41.710 --> 00:42:45.477
Fast and Broad; the values
are the program's design.

654
00:42:45.477 --> 00:42:49.978
Test is the engineering lab, with
no deferral and a two-day deadline.

655
00:42:49.978 --> 00:42:53.178
First is the pilot cohort,
about five percent.

656
00:42:53.178 --> 00:42:55.303
Fast is about a quarter of the fleet.

657
00:42:55.303 --> 00:42:58.655
Broad is everyone else,
with a seven-day deferral,

658
00:42:58.655 --> 00:43:01.840
a seven-day deadline and
a two-day grace period.

659
00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:04.898
Rings allow deferrals
of zero to thirty days,

660
00:43:04.898 --> 00:43:08.452
deadlines of two to thirty
and grace of zero to seven.

661
00:43:08.452 --> 00:43:13.695
With these values a quality update reaches
every online device within about sixteen

662
00:43:13.695 --> 00:43:18.641
days of release, and an update that breaks
Test or First can be paused before Broad.

663
00:43:18.641 --> 00:43:21.078
Feature deferral is zero everywhere,

664
00:43:21.078 --> 00:43:24.302
because a feature update
policy pins the version.

665
00:43:24.302 --> 00:43:28.891
Windows Autopatch automates ring
management, sequencing and reporting.

666
00:43:28.891 --> 00:43:34.936
For government tenants, it's
included for GCC customers with

667
00:43:34.936 --> 00:43:41.465
Microsoft 365 G3 GCC or G5 GCC, and
isn't supported in GCC High or DoD.

668
00:43:41.465 --> 00:43:46.072
Devices must be Entra joined or hybrid
joined, and co-managed devices need

669
00:43:46.072 --> 00:43:50.678
the update and device configuration
workloads at Intune or Pilot Intune.

670
00:43:50.678 --> 00:43:56.040
Autopatch creates and maintains its own
rings, so don't assign yours to its devices.

671
00:43:56.040 --> 00:43:58.612
The program's position:
use it where licensed,

672
00:43:58.612 --> 00:44:02.852
and keep the ring table as the
specification its groups are configured to.

673
00:44:02.852 --> 00:44:07.029
Version currency: 23H2
Enterprise and Education

674
00:44:07.029 --> 00:44:11.002
stop receiving updates
on November tenth, 2026.

675
00:44:11.002 --> 00:44:14.785
So the feature update
policy targets 25H2 now,

676
00:44:14.785 --> 00:44:19.130
and the compliance floor
of 24H2 rises each year.

677
00:44:19.880 --> 00:44:22.831
Three services keep their
own device inventory,

678
00:44:22.831 --> 00:44:25.192
and they disagree in predictable ways.

679
00:44:25.192 --> 00:44:28.974
Intune's managed devices drift
when devices stop checking in,

680
00:44:28.974 --> 00:44:30.679
and cleanup rules hide them.

681
00:44:30.679 --> 00:44:34.312
Entra ID accumulates stale
objects from reimaging,

682
00:44:34.312 --> 00:44:37.943
broken hybrid trust, or
devices that never enrolled.

683
00:44:37.943 --> 00:44:43.230
Defender has devices onboarded but not
enrolled, or enrolled but never onboarded.

684
00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:46.942
The program reconciles them
weekly on the Entra device ID.

685
00:44:46.942 --> 00:44:50.703
Every enrolled device must have
an Entra object with its tier,

686
00:44:50.703 --> 00:44:54.831
role and environment attributes
set, and a healthy Defender sensor.

687
00:44:54.831 --> 00:44:59.555
Every Entra object for a corporate Windows
device must have an Intune enrollment.

688
00:44:59.555 --> 00:45:04.096
A device missing from any one is a
finding, and the same comparison is the

689
00:45:04.096 --> 00:45:08.867
evidence for CM-8's requirement of a
complete inventory with no duplicates.

690
00:45:08.867 --> 00:45:10.556
Stale records need care.

691
00:45:10.556 --> 00:45:15.447
Intune cleanup rules hide devices after
thirty to two hundred seventy days,

692
00:45:15.447 --> 00:45:18.630
per platform; the program
uses ninety for Windows.

693
00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:23.718
They don't wipe or retire anything,
and hidden devices stay in Entra ID.

694
00:45:23.718 --> 00:45:27.452
Entra's last sign-in timestamp
isn't updated on every sign-in,

695
00:45:27.452 --> 00:45:31.338
so Microsoft advises not treating
anything newer than about

696
00:45:31.338 --> 00:45:34.843
twenty-one days as stale, and
disabling before deleting.

697
00:45:34.843 --> 00:45:39.318
Hybrid devices are removed in Active
Directory, and the change syncs.

698
00:45:39.318 --> 00:45:41.264
Retirement follows a fixed order,

699
00:45:41.264 --> 00:45:44.392
because the order decides
what can still be recovered.

700
00:45:44.392 --> 00:45:48.929
Record first: export the BitLocker
keys and the LAPS password.

701
00:45:48.929 --> 00:45:52.743
Retiring or deleting the Intune
object of an Entra-joined

702
00:45:52.743 --> 00:45:56.318
BitLocker device suspends
BitLocker, so back up first.

703
00:45:56.318 --> 00:45:59.624
Then act on the device:
wipe a corporate device,

704
00:45:59.624 --> 00:46:03.192
retire a personal one;
actions run at next check-in.

705
00:46:03.192 --> 00:46:06.105
Deregister from Autopilot
if it's leaving for good.

706
00:46:06.105 --> 00:46:07.917
Delete the Intune record.

707
00:46:07.917 --> 00:46:11.438
And delete the Entra object
last, after a disable period,

708
00:46:11.438 --> 00:46:14.193
because that permanently deletes its keys.

709
00:46:14.193 --> 00:46:16.666
Plan mass refreshes in daily batches:

710
00:46:16.666 --> 00:46:21.430
the tenant limit is a thousand
retires and a thousand deletes a day.

711
00:46:22.180 --> 00:46:27.216
Drift is a difference between the policy
library and a device's reported state that

712
00:46:27.216 --> 00:46:31.522
the policy should have prevented:
BitLocker off, tamper protection off,

713
00:46:31.522 --> 00:46:34.442
an OS below the floor, a
setting stuck in Conflict.

714
00:46:34.442 --> 00:46:38.471
The program detects it with the
same loop Chapter 05 describes

715
00:46:38.471 --> 00:46:41.880
for the governed path, applied
to Intune and Defender.

716
00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:46.480
A daily export of compliance,
configuration and inventory status.

717
00:46:46.480 --> 00:46:50.130
A comparison against the
template values in Chapter 08.

718
00:46:50.130 --> 00:46:53.455
Classification as a policy
gap, a device fault,

719
00:46:53.455 --> 00:46:57.568
a conflict or an inventory
mismatch, alerted by severity.

720
00:46:57.568 --> 00:47:01.111
And remediation: a device
sync or a Remediation script,

721
00:47:01.111 --> 00:47:05.993
or a policy change through change
control when the library itself is wrong.

722
00:47:05.993 --> 00:47:10.542
On the governed path every remediating
write carries an approval reference.

723
00:47:10.542 --> 00:47:13.293
Know what Log Analytics
receives, and when.

724
00:47:13.293 --> 00:47:17.475
Intune's diagnostic settings send
audit logs, operational logs,

725
00:47:17.475 --> 00:47:21.893
compliance organizational logs
and the Intune devices inventory.

726
00:47:21.893 --> 00:47:24.868
Audit and operational logs
are sent immediately.

727
00:47:24.868 --> 00:47:30.177
Compliance and inventory are exported once
every twenty-four hours and can take up

728
00:47:30.177 --> 00:47:35.179
to forty-eight to arrive, and Microsoft
says not to rely on a fixed delivery time.

729
00:47:35.179 --> 00:47:39.318
So those tables are for trends and
evidence, not minute-level alerts.

730
00:47:39.318 --> 00:47:42.534
And per-setting configuration
status isn't among them,

731
00:47:42.534 --> 00:47:46.680
so the configuration comparison
comes from Microsoft Graph reports.

732
00:47:46.680 --> 00:47:50.571
Three query pitfalls: don't
hard-code compliance state strings,

733
00:47:50.571 --> 00:47:54.687
don't join the operational logs on
a device ID column it doesn't have,

734
00:47:54.687 --> 00:47:57.755
and don't look for
configuration conflicts there.

735
00:47:57.755 --> 00:47:59.330
The alerts follow from that.

736
00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:03.742
Fleet-wide noncompliance above ten
percent comes from the daily export.

737
00:48:03.742 --> 00:48:07.485
But Tier 0 noncompliance is
polled from Graph hourly:

738
00:48:07.485 --> 00:48:11.142
from the export, the alert
would arrive two days late.

739
00:48:11.142 --> 00:48:18.370
Meanwhile Conditional Access blocks the
device, because CP-002 has no grace period.

740
00:48:19.120 --> 00:48:20.995
Last, what the assessor reads.

741
00:48:20.995 --> 00:48:25.932
The program's evidence is the records
the services keep, exported on a schedule

742
00:48:25.932 --> 00:48:30.945
and retained, for the configuration
management and flaw remediation controls.

743
00:48:30.945 --> 00:48:38.237
The control wording is from NIST SP
800-53 Revision 5, release 5.2.0,

744
00:48:38.237 --> 00:48:44.257
which FedRAMP's 2026 catalog uses without
adding parameter values to these controls.

745
00:48:44.257 --> 00:48:49.424
CM-2, baseline configuration: the
policy library exported from Graph,

746
00:48:49.424 --> 00:48:52.958
under version control,
with its review history.

747
00:48:52.958 --> 00:48:56.467
CM-6, configuration
settings: per-setting status,

748
00:48:56.467 --> 00:49:02.258
the exception register with approvals,
and the Intune audit log of every change.

749
00:49:02.258 --> 00:49:06.902
CM-7, least functionality: attack
surface reduction in block,

750
00:49:06.902 --> 00:49:10.895
the firewall rule set, the
Edge extension allow list.

751
00:49:10.895 --> 00:49:15.182
CM-8, inventory: the
weekly reconciliation.

752
00:49:15.182 --> 00:49:20.657
SI-2, flaw remediation: the ring
table and per-ring compliance.

753
00:49:20.657 --> 00:49:24.074
SI-2's defined period is the
organization's parameter,

754
00:49:24.074 --> 00:49:26.482
and the ring deadlines are what set it.

755
00:49:26.482 --> 00:49:30.557
Two GCC facts change where
data and endpoints live.

756
00:49:30.557 --> 00:49:36.296
Intune in a GCC tenant isn't a separate
government instance: GCC uses the

757
00:49:36.296 --> 00:49:42.619
commercial service and worldwide Graph,
and only GCC High and DoD are separate.

758
00:49:42.619 --> 00:49:47.660
Defender for Endpoint for US Government
is built in Azure US Government,

759
00:49:47.660 --> 00:49:51.356
with the GCC portal at security
dot microsoft dot com.

760
00:49:51.356 --> 00:49:56.984
And Windows Update for Business reports
don't meet GCC compliance requirements,

761
00:49:56.984 --> 00:50:00.932
so SI-2 evidence comes from
Intune and Autopatch reports.

762
00:50:00.932 --> 00:50:06.399
The program also maps this evidence to
FedRAMP 20x Key Security Indicators,

763
00:50:06.399 --> 00:50:11.295
as its own claim, written for cloud
service offerings, not an agency fleet.

764
00:50:11.295 --> 00:50:16.875
So, from Monday: inventory and triage every
GPO; set the tenant compliance defaults;

765
00:50:16.875 --> 00:50:20.978
carry procurement into the group
tag; onboard servers with a

766
00:50:20.978 --> 00:50:25.820
short-lived certificate principal;
and reconcile inventories weekly.

767
00:50:25.820 --> 00:50:30.095
Chapters 04 to 10 and
Appendix A carry the detail.

768
00:50:30.095 --> 00:50:30.930
Thank you.
